WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.940
Good morning Hank it's Monday

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:03.420
I'm a man, I'm a zebra I'm a...  man bra

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.320
Oh wait that's something else

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.860
So Hank I'm here in the great city of Chicago

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.740
at a very crowded English teachers' conference

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.900
which is a lot of fun because

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.800
A. I get to talk with English teachers about books; one of my favourite endeavours and

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.620
B. I was starting to miss signing my name

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.500
But before that, and before the man bra

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.560
and before a bath so luxurious and wasteful that it made me wonder whether luxurious and wasteful are synonyms

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.280
and before we were coming up the elevator with Henry battling the oppression of strollers

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.460
and before I found myself at the bean

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.280
and before I ate a breakfast at a restaurant

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.640
where the hostess was a nerdfighter

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.780
and before I was reminded of the many and very miseries of Chicago traffic

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.880
before all that I was at Brilliance Audio, my audio book publisher

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.280
narrating The Fault In Our Stars

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.760
So there will be 2 audiobook versions:

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.020
one real audiobook narrated by Kate Rudd

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:45.980
pictured here

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.880
greeting her adoring public in a Panera

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.600
and one narrated by me

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.760
Kate sounds exactly like Hazel

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.500
and her recording is brilliant

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.400
I sound like a thirty four year old man making for some awkward moments

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.680
here's a sample: "Chapter 6"

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.360
Actually no, we must avoid all spoilers

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.920
my recording will be available only in a physical, limited edition boxset available at dtfba.com

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.040
Kate's recording will be the real audiobook and available everywhere

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.960
the reason it's possible to have 2 audiobooks

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.260
one released by dftba records

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.840
and one released by Brilliance

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.440
is that they worked with me to share the audio publishing rights in a really cool and innovative way

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.040
and when copyright law works, that's how it works

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.400
nice people, sometimes with lawyers, get together and figure out what's fair

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.200
So Hank in your last video you talked about copyright and intellectual property

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.720
and correctly pointed out that the measures currently before congress on this topic are a steaming hot slice of crazy

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.200
which is now apparently a vegetable

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.460
but that said, copyright is really complex

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.660
like obviously you're not violating my copyright if you post a quote from my book on your Tumblr

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.560
and obviously you are violating my copyright if you post my book on your Tumblr

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.200
but the question of where you draw that line is really complicated and ill-defined

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.440
side note: a lot of people will say that creative commons can bypass all of these problems

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.600
but I don't think so

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.000
I'm a big fan of creative commons and Hank you and I have both released a lot of stuff

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.140
from songs to zombie apocalypse novellas, via creative commons licenses

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.800
but I just don't think it's practical for everything

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.280
Okay so let me give you an example of the complexity of fair use and the kind of chilling effect it can have on discourse

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.040
In 2004 I was finishing a novel called Looking for Alaska

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:14.940
and the nice lawyers at penguin decided the character Alaska quotes too much of an Edna St Vincent Millay poem

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.060
The poem begins: Night falls fast

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.460
Today is in the past

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.120
Blown from the dark hill, 
Hither to my door

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.040
Three flakes,
Then four arrive

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.660
then many more

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.440
they decided under fair use I could only quote two lines of the poem

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.440
and that if I wanted to quote more

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.420
I would have to pay Edna St Vincent Millay's estate

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.020
which I would've done except I didn't have any money

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.720
So Hank in the same situation today I would just pay

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.920
and use the quote and it would be fine

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.740
because now, thanks to Copyright, I've sold some books and I have enough money to pay for that kind of thing

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:47.380
So one of my big issues with copyright law is that currently it privileges  people like 2011 me

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.880
who have $500 or whatever to pay Edna St Vincent Millay's estate

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.440
and it punishes people like 2004 me who don't have that money

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.380
and the really terrifying thing to me about the legislation currently before congress

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.700
is that it would increase that inequality

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.180
YouTube and Google and Tumblr can afford lawyers to fight lawsuits from viacom

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.020
but I can't

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.140
meanwhile this legislation will do nothing to stop wholesale piracy

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.040
because it is already illegal

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.980
Hank, in the end it only made my book a little bit worse to lose those lines

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.720
and I'm gonna disagree with the alarmists

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.400
I suspect that if SOPA were to pass it would only make the internet a little bit worse

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.960
but the internet doesn't need to be worse

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.260
it needs to be better

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.280
and I don't think SOPA is pushing us in the right direction at all

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.900
So Hank, that's my take. And you can trust me, I'm a man bra

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.300
Nerdfighters lets continue the conversation in comments

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.040
Hank, I will see you on Wednesday!

